<details class="studio-shell studio-guide mb-4" data-studio-guide>
    <summary class="studio-disclosure-summary">
        <span class="flex min-w-0 items-center gap-3"><span class="studio-guide-icon" aria-hidden="true">?</span><span><strong>How Research Studio works</strong><span class="block text-xs font-normal text-slate-500">A guide from your first question to your finished document</span></span></span>
        <span class="studio-disclosure-hint">Open guide</span>
    </summary>
    <div class="studio-guide-content">
        <div class="studio-guide-start"><p class="font-bold text-slate-900">Start here</p><ol><li><strong>Plan:</strong> choose your project type and research design, then record your objectives.</li><li><strong>Build evidence:</strong> keep sources, instruments and decisions in your project library.</li><li><strong>Write:</strong> choose a chapter, draft it, and check that your changes are saved.</li><li><strong>Review &amp; export:</strong> address feedback, check requirements and preview the complete document.</li></ol></div>
        <p class="text-xs leading-6 text-slate-500">Open a topic below for practical steps. Available tools depend on your project type, enabled features and your role.</p>
        <div class="studio-guide-topics">
            <details><summary>Projects, setup &amp; control center</summary><p>Create a Research or Final-Year project. Choose a research design that fits your question, add your title and objectives, then review the setup before creating it. The control center brings together your project progress, current workflow stage, timeline and next actions. Use Continue writing to return to your document.</p></details>
            <details><summary>Chapters, writing &amp; focus mode</summary><p>Open Chapter navigator and choose a section. The navigator closes on the selected chapter to give your editor more room; open it again to switch. Use the toolbar for headings, lists, links and tables. To delete an entire table, click inside one of its cells, then choose Remove table. Use Ctrl/Cmd+Z to undo a removal. Writing tools holds the chapter checklist, heading insertion and available reference or dataset tools. Focus writing hides the surrounding panels; use Exit focus or Escape to return.</p></details>
            <details><summary>Autosave, versions &amp; recovering work</summary><p>Changes save automatically after you pause typing. Watch the save status below the editor and use Save version to save deliberately. Section version history lets you inspect older drafts and restore one as a new version. If saving pauses or you are offline, keep the tab open and copy your draft before reloading. A newer save in another tab pauses this tab's saves so it cannot overwrite that work.</p></details>
            <details><summary>Plan, library &amp; institutional guidelines</summary><p>In Plan &amp; library, record your questions, methodological decisions and formatting preferences. Add a worksheet, instrument, proposal or attachment, then select it to edit. Resources with an editor keep recoverable versions. Save a resource before inserting its saved version into a chapter. For an uploaded guideline, compare extracted headings with the original and confirm them before applying the structure. Archiving preserves the resource.</p></details>
            <details><summary>Literature, review notes &amp; citations</summary><p>Use Literature &amp; references to discover papers, save references and record your own review notes. Mark a paper Reviewed when its notes are ready. In the literature chapter, Writing tools can insert citations and your reviewed notes; the references section can insert a reviewed bibliography. Citation and bibliography formatting follows your project's selected style. Check source metadata and inserted wording against the paper.</p></details>
            <details><summary>Methods, coding, sample planning &amp; data</summary><p>Plan &amp; library contains research design requirements, qualitative coding and sample-size planning. Coding connects anonymised excerpts to a source, locator, code, theme and memo. Sample planning saves the assumptions behind its calculation. Where enabled, CSV analysis computes summaries from supplied observations, while chapter dataset tools attach supporting files with a chosen access level. Use the tools that fit your design.</p></details>
            <details><summary>Research Assistant &amp; writing suggestions</summary><p>Use Research Assistant to ask about your project or current section. The review prompts in Writing tools provide starting questions. Where enabled, editor suggestions can help with wording. Review suggestions before inserting them, support claims with your own sources, and supply actual evidence for analysis. Assistant replies do not approve chapters or replace a supervisor's review.</p></details>
            <details><summary>Comments, supervision &amp; corrections</summary><p>Open Comments and corrections beneath the editor to discuss the selected chapter. Reviewers can request specific corrections and approve sections when their permissions allow it. In an institutional project, supervision tools show an accepted supervisor or a pending invitation. Respond to feedback in the relevant chapter and save the revised version before requesting another review.</p></details>
            <details><summary>Validation, workflow &amp; publication</summary><p>Run validation from Project overview or the control center to review the checks available for your project. Read the findings alongside your institution's requirements. Workflow actions move the project between configured stages when your role allows them. If publication is enabled and you have permission, Create linked publication creates a moderated Knowledge Hub draft linked to the source project.</p></details>
            <details><summary>Preview, formatting &amp; document export</summary><p>Choose citation style, font, spacing and margins in Plan &amp; library. Preview / export shows the assembled research document. Download DOCX for further editing or PDF for a fixed layout. Large exports open a progress page while the document is prepared; download it when ready, or retry if preparation fails. Check the assembled chapters and references before submission.</p></details>
            <details><summary>Specialized workspaces</summary><p>Projects configured for SIWES or seminar work can show a Specialized workspace. Use its project-specific records and actions alongside the main plan, writing and review tools. If the link is absent, that workspace is not enabled for this project.</p></details>
        </div>
    </div>
</details>
